From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tcdent <tcdent@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:19:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 30/38] fs: sharedfs: key window chunks by inode, not slot

The chunk table keyed chunks by index slot alone. A file unlinked while
still mmapped keeps its guest inode, but the host frees its slot at once,
and the next file created can reuse it. That file's writes then found the
old file's chunk and went into the unlinked file's pages: they read back
until the old mapping was torn down, after which the new file held zeros.
The old inode's teardown also released every chunk under the slot, the
new file's included, and reclaim found "the inode behind a chunk" through
the slot's current generation, missing the old file's mappings. Writes
that needed more space than the old file had mapped spun in
dax_iomap_rw.

Chunks are now keyed by inode number, which carries the generation, and
the inode behind a chunk is looked up by that number directly.

Reported-by: tablet.kernel@a10k.internal
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SKmspe5nVbuvzdvYZa9UhW
---
